#
# Git applies the LAST matching line per attribute, so the blocks below
# run general -> specific with the frozen-corpus block last: nothing
# above it can reach app/, samples/ or diagrams/.
#
# There is deliberately NO blanket `* text=auto`. The frozen trees are
# predominantly LF, but eleven members are CRLF-terminated - the five
# under app/ named above plus six under samples/ - and a blanket rule
# would renormalise all eleven on the next commit, corrupting the
# reference of record and breaking the fixed-width record offsets the
# migration is validated against. For the same reason, never run
# `git add --renormalize`. Every rule here is bound to an explicit
# pattern, and `git diff -- app samples diagrams` must stay empty.
#
# These attributes govern content conversion only; they do not set the
# executable bit, which is file mode recorded on the files themselves.
# To check one path: `git check-attr text eol binary -- <path>`.

# Java sources, build descriptors, resources and docs: LF in the index,
# checked out as LF.
*.java          text eol=lf
*.xml           text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.properties    text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf

# ==================================================================
# PRE-EXISTING DOCUMENTATION - NARROWS THE `*.md` RULE ABOVE
#
# README.md is not a migration artefact. It existed at the anchor commit
# 7756d89 and it is the ONLY text file outside app/ and samples/ that is
# CRLF-terminated there: 324 lines, 14639 bytes, blob
# 0d7292ad44dd150b089e41d6a630eefe95857f6e. It is also the authoritative legacy
# transaction, program and JCL inventory, so it is reference material first and
# an edit target second.
#
# Without the `-text` exemption below, the `*.md text eol=lf` rule above matches
# it and strips every CR byte, producing a whole-file diff with ZERO content
# change - exactly the sweeping, review-obscuring diff CONTRIBUTING.md:33 asks
# contributors not to create, and a continuity break against the anchor for a
# file no migration step needs to reformat. `-text` restores byte-for-byte
# passthrough in both
# directions, matching how the frozen corpus is protected. Diffs still render as
# text; only EOL conversion is disabled.
#
# `-text` alone is sufficient, because Git ignores `eol` when `text` is unset.
# `!eol` is added only so the inherited `*.md` value stops being reported at all,
# and `git check-attr text eol -- README.md` answers `unset` and `unspecified`
# rather than the misleading pair `unset` and `lf`.
#
# This is a NARROWING exemption, not a licence to reformat: appending the Java
# build and run sections that the plan requires stays permitted, and any such
# append must simply use CRLF to match the surrounding file.
#
# The other pre-existing Markdown files - CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, CONTRIBUTING.md,
# docs/index.md, docs/project-guide.md and docs/technical-specifications.md -
# are all LF at the anchor, so `*.md text eol=lf` is a no-op for them and they
# need no exemption. Re-check with the command below before adding any pattern.
#
#   git show 7756d89:<path> | file -        # LF or CRLF at the anchor?
# ==================================================================
README.md       -text !eol

# ---- Unix launchers and shell scripts: LF, or the shell cannot execute them.
# ---- mvnw is extensionless, so it needs a rule of its own.
*.sh            text eol=lf
mvnw            text eol=lf

# ---- The Maven launcher's repository-controlled JVM options file. It carries no
# ---- extension and is anchored with a slash, so this pattern resolves from the
# ---- repository root only, never from a nested .mvn directory. Pinning the
# ---- ending is functional rather than cosmetic: every line is handed to the JVM
# ---- verbatim as a raw command-line option - bin/mvn reads it through
# ---- `tr -s '\r\n' '  '` into MAVEN_OPTS and bin/mvn.cmd reads it with a
# ---- `for /F` loop - so its exact bytes reach the launcher and must not depend
# ---- on the checking-out platform or on a developer's core.autocrlf setting.
.mvn/jvm.config text eol=lf

# Container and repository-hygiene files with no usable extension.
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
.editorconfig   text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Windows launchers keep CRLF, which cmd.exe requires for reliable
# parsing. This covers mvnw.cmd; a second literal rule for it could
# only drift out of step with this one.
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never EOL-converted and never diffed as text.
# `binary` is Git's own built-in macro for `-text -diff`.
*.zip           binary
*.tar           binary
*.gz            binary
*.tgz           binary
*.7z            binary
*.jar           binary
*.war           binary
*.class         binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.ttf           binary
*.otf           binary
*.eot           binary
*.PS            binary

# ---- Fixed-width test fixtures copied from app/data/ASCII/. These are the
# ---- parity inputs, and their geometry is load-bearing: acctdata.txt is 50
# ---- records of 300 bytes, custdata.txt 50 of 500, discgrp.txt 51 of 50,
# ---- trancatg.txt 18 of 60 and trantype.txt 7 of 60, each terminated by a
# ---- single LF. Without an explicit rule `git check-attr` reports text and eol
# ---- as unspecified, so a clone made with core.autocrlf=true would add one CR
# ---- per record and shift every offset a reader computes - silently, because
# ---- the byte count still divides evenly by nothing the test asserts.
# ----
# ---- The pattern is PATH-SCOPED rather than a blanket `*.txt`, and it sits
# ---- above the frozen-corpus block, so it can never reach app/catlg/LISTCAT.txt
# ---- or any other .txt under app/. `src/test/resources/**/*.txt` matches files
# ---- directly in that directory as well as in any sub-directory, so a fixture
# ---- added under a nested baseline folder inherits the same guarantee.
src/test/resources/**/*.txt  text eol=lf

# ---- The SAME fixtures additionally need a narrow whitespace exemption, and
# ---- the reason is that their trailing spaces are DATA, not sloppiness. A
# ---- fixed-width record pads its short fields to width, so a record whose last
# ---- field is short necessarily ends in spaces before the LF - which is the
# ---- case for every record of acctdata.txt, carddata.txt, custdata.txt and
# ---- dailytran.txt, whose widths the frozen copybooks in app/cpy fix.
# ----
# ---- With `whitespace` unspecified, git's default `blank-at-eol` rule reports
# ---- every one of those as an error, so `git diff --check` exits non-zero and
# ---- `git apply --whitespace=error` refuses the patch, on files that are
# ---- byte-correct. The trap is what a contributor does next: the obvious fix
# ---- is to strip the trailing spaces, which silently shortens those records and
# ---- breaks every offset a fixed-width reader computes. So the exemption is
# ---- not a convenience - it removes the incentive to corrupt the data.
# ----
# ---- `whitespace=-blank-at-eol` unsets ONLY that one rule. space-before-tab,
# ---- indent-with-non-tab, tab-in-indent and cr-at-eol all remain in force, so
# ---- a genuine CR or a stray tab in a fixture is still an error. A blanket
# ---- `-whitespace` would have disabled all of them and is deliberately not
# ---- used. The pattern is path-scoped and sits above the frozen-corpus block,
# ---- exactly as the eol rule above does, so it cannot reach app/.
src/test/resources/**/*.txt  whitespace=-blank-at-eol

# ==================================================================
# FROZEN LEGACY CORPUS - LAST BLOCK, SO IT OVERRIDES EVERY RULE ABOVE
#
# app/ holds the COBOL programs, copybooks, BMS mapsets, JCL members, PROC and
# CTL members, the CICS CSD, the VSAM catalogue listing and the data fixtures.
# samples/ holds the z/OS compile templates, the build procedures and two
# binary runtime archives. diagrams/ holds the legacy illustrations. All three
# are read-only reference material and must survive both checkout and commit
# byte-for-byte, so Git is told to perform no text detection, no EOL conversion
# and no diff-time transformation on them.
# ==================================================================
app/**          -text
samples/**      -text
diagrams/**     -text

# The EBCDIC datasets are fixed-width records with no line terminators
# at all, so Git's NUL-byte heuristic would misclassify most of them as
# text. Marked binary explicitly and last, because the `*.PS` pattern
# above does not match AWS.M2.CARDDEMO.DALYTRAN.PS.INIT.
app/data/EBCDIC/**  binary
